/* ============================================================================
   divisor-sum.epher -- the sum of the divisors of n, sigma(n)

   Sums all positive divisors of n. The def pairdiv(n, i) walks the trial
   divisors up to the square root and adds each divisor and its partner
   n / i together (a square adds its root once), which is the classroom
   method; epher has no divisor-sum builtin (ndivisors counts, factors
   only displays). The classic check: the divisors of 12 are 1, 2, 3, 4,
   6 and 12, and they add to 28.

   Source: the divisor function sigma(n), Hardy and Wright, "An
   Introduction to the Theory of Numbers", ch. 16, section 16.7. Results
   are exact; the recursion depth limits the def to n of a few thousand,
   which is plenty for the checks below.

   Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/number-theory/divisors/divisor-sum.epher"
   In the REPL:             load epher scripts/number-theory/divisors/divisor-sum.epher
   Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter

   There is no knob: the sample numbers are the point of the script.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- the divisor-sum def ----------------------------------------------------------
// pairdiv(n, i) adds i and n / i when i divides n, walking i = 1, 2, ...
// until i * i > n; a perfect square adds its root i exactly once.
def pairdiv(n, i) = if i * i > n then 0 else if mod(n, i) == 0 then (if i * i == n then i + pairdiv(n, i + 1) else i + n / i + pairdiv(n, i + 1)) else pairdiv(n, i + 1)
def sigma(n) = pairdiv(n, 1)

// ---- the checks ---------------------------------------------------------------------
for x in {6, 12, 24, 36, 100} do sigma(x)
// 12 = 2^2 * 3, and the multiplicative formula reads
// sigma(12) = (1 + 2 + 4) * (1 + 3) = 7 * 4 = 28 (Hardy and Wright, 16.7).
(1 + 2 + 4) * (1 + 3)

// ---- cross-check by direct addition -------------------------------------------------
// Sum the divisors of 12 one by one with a packed while loop; it must
// agree with the def above.
v = {1, 0}
while v[1] <= 12 do v = {v[1] + 1, v[2] + (if mod(12, v[1]) == 0 then v[1] else 0)}
print("divisors of 12 add to:", v[2])
print("sigma(12):", sigma(12))
print("sigma(360):", sigma(360))            // 15 * 13 * 6 = 1170
/* ---- expected output ----
= {12, 28, 60, 91, 217}
= 28
= {1, 0}
= divisors of 12 add to: 28
= sigma(12): 28
= sigma(360): 1170
*/
